4. Score System

A new class named Score.java was created to manage the player's score.

The main methods are:

public void increaseScore()

This method increases the score by one.

public int getScore()

This method returns the current score.

public void resetScore()

This method resets the score to zero when the player dies.

The score is increased when the player moves upward:

if (keyH.up) {
    player.y -= player.tileSize;
    score.increaseScore();
    keyH.up = false;
}

The score is displayed on the game screen using:

g.drawString("Score: " + score.getScore(), 20, 30);


---

5. Procedural Map Generation

A new class named LevelGenerator.java was created.

The generator randomly produces two types of sections:

0 → Road
1 → River

Random generation is implemented using Java's Random class.

The generated section is then passed to the TileManager:

int section = levelGenerator.generateSection();

tileM.generateSection(section);

This allows the game to generate different road and river sections during gameplay.


---

6. TileManager Integration

The TileManager was modified to support section generation.

The following method was added:

public void generateSection(int section)

If the generated section is 0, the selected row becomes a road.

If the generated section is 1, the selected row becomes a river.

This connects the procedural level generator with the existing game map.


---

7. Player Progress Tracking

The player's Y-position is used to detect forward movement.

A variable named:

int lastPlayerY = 300;

was added to keep track of the previous player position.

The game checks:

if (player.y < lastPlayerY)

When the player moves forward, a new section is generated and the previous position is updated.

This provides basic progress tracking and procedural section generation.


---

8. Increasing Difficulty

To make the game progressively harder, the speed of cars and logs was increased according to the player's score.

Score below 5

The game uses the normal speed.

Score 5 or above

car1.setSpeed(3);
car2.setSpeed(4);
car3.setSpeed(5);
car4.setSpeed(6);

log1.speed = 3;
log2.speed = -4;

Score 10 or above

car1.setSpeed(4);
car2.setSpeed(5);
car3.setSpeed(6);
car4.setSpeed(7);

log1.speed = 4;
log2.speed = -5;

Therefore, as the player progresses, avoiding cars and staying on logs becomes more difficult.


---

9. Score Reset

When the player dies because of a car collision or falling into the river, the score is reset:

score.resetScore();

The player is then returned to the starting position:

player.x = 380;
player.y = 300;

This ensures that each new attempt starts with a score of zero.


